<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumbakonam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumbakonam is a temple town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the Kaveri and Arasalar rivers. An important centre under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> of Thanjavur, it is known for its many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples and for the Mahamaham festival, held at its sacred tank every twelve years. It was long a centre of learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumbakonam.jpg" alt="Kumbakonam" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trawell.in · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbakonam_Mahamaham_Tank.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kumbakonam, also called Kudanthai, lies about 40 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and 282 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, in the old delta region watered by the Kaveri and its tributaries. It is bounded by the Kaveri to the north and the Arasalar to the south and is the second-largest city of the district. Its name, roughly Pot's Corner, recalls the mythical pot of the creator god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, which held the seeds of all living beings. Tradition holds that it was displaced during a cosmic dissolution and came to rest at the site of the city, an event commemorated in the Mahamaham festival held every twelve years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was inhabited in the Sangam period. Kumbakonam is identified with the ancient town of Kudavayil, where the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king Karikala held court, and it is linked with Malaikūrram, a Chola capital in the 7th century CE. According to the Sinnamanur plates, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimara-srivallabha">Srimara Srivallabha</a> defeated a confederacy of Pallavas, Cholas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a> there in 859. The region grew in importance under the medieval Cholas between the 9th and 12th centuries. The Pandyas took the town in 1290, and after their decline in the 14th century it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. The Vijayanagara emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> visited in 1524 and bathed in the Mahamaham tank.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and Thanjavur Nayaks ruled the town from 1535 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> annexed it in 1673. After the fall of Vijayanagara in 1565 poets, musicians and scholars came to the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s invasion in 1784 caused heavy economic disruption, from which recovery took until the early 19th century. In 1799 the Thanjavur Maratha ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfoji-ii">Serfoji II</a> ceded Kumbakonam to the British East India Company. It was made a municipality in 1866 and became a municipal corporation on 24 August 2021.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kumbakonam is known as the temple city, with about 188 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples within the municipal limits. The Adi Kumbeswarar Temple is its oldest Shaivaite shrine, with origins in the 7th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> period, and the Sarangapani temple, with a twelve-tiered gateway tower of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> period, is its largest Vaishnavite shrine and one of the 108 Divya Desams. At nearby Darasuram the Airavatesvara Temple of the 12th century, built by Rajaraja Chola II, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p>Under British rule the city became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scholarship and Western education, which earned it the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>. The Government Arts College, founded as a provincial school in 1854, counted the mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan among its alumni, and the Tamil scholar U. V. Swaminatha Iyer taught there. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the city 140,156 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumbakonam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
